    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Uzbekistan declared independence on 31 August 1991 after the failed coup in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam-karimov">Islam Karimov</a>, Communist first secretary since 1989, ruled it as an authoritarian president until his death in September 2016.</li><li>The economy rests on commodities: in 2011 Uzbekistan was the world's seventh-largest producer and fifth-largest exporter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and its seventh-largest gold producer, while Soviet-era plants and ample gas make it the largest electricity producer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shavkat-mirziyoyev">Shavkat Mirziyoyev</a>, president from December 2016, made the currency fully convertible in September 2017, abolished Soviet-style exit visas and mended relations with every neighbour, though V-Dem still classed the state a closed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autocracy">autocracy</a> in 2024.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Uzbekistan is one of only two doubly landlocked countries in the world, the other being Liechtenstein: none of its rivers reaches the sea, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aral-sea">Aral Sea</a> on its northern edge, once the fourth-largest inland sea on Earth, has shrunk to about a tenth of its former area since the 1960s.</p></aside>
    </section>

